{
  "description": "Match response shapes to what Resend sends. Live responses (2026-10-29) and resend-node, the maintained official SDK, disagree with the spec: createApiKey, createDomain, getBroadcast and sendBroadcast also return `object`, and createSegment returns `object` and the new segment's `name`. deleteContact returns the contact id as `contact`, not `id`. Adding a contact to a segment returns `{ id, audienceId }` and removing one returns `{ object, id, audienceId, deleted }`, where `id` is the contact and `audienceId` (camelCase on the wire) the segment; the spec's `contact_id` and `segment_id` never appear. listContactTopics items carry `created_at`, and updateContactTopics answers `{ object, id }` with the contact id. getEmail and listEmails always carry `scheduled_at`, and getEmail also returns `tags` and `topic_id`. updateEmail and cancelEmail return `{ object, id }`, not the request options or the full email. A permissive batch returns the rejected items in `errors`. getEmailMetrics rows carry each requested metric beside the dimension columns, as resend-node's `EmailMetricsDataRow` types them. getReceivingEmail adds `html_format`, `authentication` (SPF, DKIM, DMARC results) and `raw` (a signed download link), live with a verified domain. Responses keep the deprecated `audience_id`, which older records still carry.\n\nA webhook's `status` is `enabled` or `disabled`. updateWebhook's request already takes that enum, but getWebhook and listWebhooks return `status` as a bare string. Live getWebhook and listWebhooks responses (2026-10-29) returned `enabled`, and `disabled` after an updateWebhook with `status: \"disabled\"`. Both responses now use the WebhookStatus enum.",
